  "answer_text": "Great question \u2014 and it's actually a surprisingly contentious debate, much like \"GIF\"! Here's a breakdown:\n\n---\n\n## How to Pronounce \"Doge\" \ud83d\udc15\n\n### The Most Common / \"Officially\" Accepted Pronunciation:\n**\"DOHJ\"** (rhymes with *vogue*, but with a soft J/ZH sound at the end)\n- IPA: **/do\u028a\u0292/**\n\nThis is considered the correct pronunciation of Dogecoin \u2014 \"dohj coin\" \u2014 and was confirmed by Dogecoin's co-creator Jackson Palmer, who said the name was inspired by the animated series *Homestar Runner*, in which a character spells out D-O-G-E and another reads it as \"dohj.\"\n\n---\n\n### The Main Pronunciation Variants:\n\nAccording to Wikipedia, \"doge\" (the meme) can be pronounced **/do\u028a\u0292/**, **/do\u028ad\u0292/**, or **/do\u028a\u0261/**.\n\nHere's a quick guide to each:\n\n| Pronunciation | Sounds Like | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **DOHZH** (/do\u028a\u0292/) | Like \"beige\" but starting with \"do-\" | Most commonly heard in media; the \"official\" pick |\n| **DOHJ** (/do\u028ad\u0292/) | Rhymes with \"lodge\" | Also very common; preferred by many internet users |\n| **DOHG** (/do\u028a\u0261/) | Rhymes with \"rogue\" | Hard G; based on standard English vowel rules (\"dog\" + silent E = \"doge\") |\n| **DOH-geh** | Two syllables, like \"doggy\" | Some people use this with a long O, hard G, and spoken \"e\" sound, arguing it sounds like a bastardized version of \"doggy.\" |\n\n---\n\n### Why Is There So Much Confusion?\n\nSince \"Doge\" is a word that was practically made up for the purposes of an internet meme, it's no surprise that there's a lot of confusion about how to pronounce it.\n\nThe word came out of the animal meme \"lolspeak\" of the early internet era, where the conceit was that animals knew English but were still learning to spell and hadn't yet mastered irregular grammar.\n\n---\n\n### The Bottom Line:\n\nIf you want to say it the way the **creators intended**, go with **\"DOHJ\"** or **\"DOHZH\"** (soft G). If you apply standard English spelling rules (like \"mope\" from \"mop\"), you'd say **\"DOHG\"** (rhymes with rogue). All are widely understood \u2014 so like the GIF debate, there's no single universally agreed-upon answer!",
